      "writtenOn": "2022-04-18T20:20:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Should this be gated on whether a PD 3.0 port partner is currently connected on a port?",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79b7949a_063dc5fe",
        "filename": "common/usb_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1059,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-18T23:25:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Unless there\u0027s some alternative action to take with a PD 2.0 connection, it seems easier to just fire and forget to the PE, and the PE can ignore the request if it\u0027s 2.0.",
      "parentUuid": "aabc07e2_b1ca6589",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "111bf89b_30578206",
        "filename": "common/usb_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1059,
      "author": {
        "id": 1527433
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-22T19:21:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Alerts were added in USB PD Rev 3.0, so there is no alternate action for PD 2.0 connections.",
      "parentUuid": "79b7949a_063dc5fe",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "69f2e1ca_0f101ac3",
        "filename": "common/usb_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1060,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-18T23:25:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Does this need to be synchronized?",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "124139f0_d4bdff36",
        "filename": "common/usb_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1060,
      "author": {
        "id": 1527433
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-22T19:21:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think it needs to be. The only other example I found of looping over ports and sending pd_dpm_requests is `pd_chipset_startup` in usb_tc_drp_acc_trysrc_sm.c. There, the only condition for the pd_dpm_request is that `CONFIG_USB_PE_SM` is enabled.\n\nBut, better safe than sorry so I added a few mutexes to avoid any race conditions. I setup one for each port, similar to `pd_set_vbus_discharge` earlier in this file.",
      "parentUuid": "69f2e1ca_0f101ac3",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9c0fdc23_75cdd45e",
        "filename": "common/usb_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1060,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-26T02:38:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I wasn\u0027t worried about the DPM request. That\u0027s an atomic bit set and shouldn\u0027t need additional synchronization.\n\nBut `pe_set_ado` is plain old `int` assignment. I don\u0027t know that we can count on those being atomic, and conceivably, you could have this function called from multiple places and get some unexpected value. Even if you avoided corruption, overlapping calls would also cause a related but probably not as bad issue where the first ADO to be written is lost, and we only ever send the second. If you anticipate this function being called from multiple places for unrelated reasons, you might want to have some mechanism to make the caller wait when there is already an Alert in progress.\n\nFor now, I think this issue is mostly theoretical, and it\u0027s probably sufficient to do your mutexing inside `pe_set_ado` and make the mutex part of the port state. The current approach is also OK if you replicate it in `pd_send_alert_msg`.",
      "parentUuid": "124139f0_d4bdff36",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "aa4371f0_e0ecabe5",
        "filename": "common/usb_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1060,
      "author": {
        "id": 1527433
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-27T00:30:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, I understand. I added mutexing to the port state and lock/unlock it using new pe_lock_ado and pe_unlock_ado functions from pe_send_alert_message.\n\nBut, you are right. There is still a possible issue where sending two alerts right next to each other could lead to the second ADO overwriting the first. Testing this on a Brya, there was about a 3 ms window between when the mutex was unlocked and the mode entry function read the PE state\u0027s ADO.\n\nI don\u0027t think it is currently reasonable to add the mutex unlock later, like when the ADO is cleared in `pe_send_alert_run`. That function is never reached for ports with nothing connected.",
      "parentUuid": "9c0fdc23_75cdd45e",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ad5e6bdd_5b4c19b1",
        "filename": "common/usb_common.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 1060,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-04-27T21:54:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "At the risk of being tedious about this, I don\u0027t love exposing the locking mechanism in the PE\u0027s API, because then it\u0027s the responsibility of the caller to use it correctly. The current code also doesn\u0027t synchronize the reads.\n\nHow about something like this?\n\n* `pe_set_ado`:\n1. Locks the mutex\n2. Checks if the ADO is currently unset\n3. If the ADO is 0, sets the ADO; if the ADO is already set, don\u0027t change it\n4. Unlock the mutex\n5. Return success if the ADO was successfully set and some failure code if it was unchanged\n\n* `pd_send_alert_msg` (and any other users of this feature) call `pe_set_ado` and check the return value. If it makes sense, I guess they should wait and retry on failure, but it would probably be fine if the caller just dropped it. The partner should do a Get_Status anyway.\n\n* `pe_send_alert_entry`\n1. Locks the mutex\n2. Reads the ADO\n3. Unlocks the mutex\n\n* `pe_send_alert_run`, after sending\n1. Locks the mutex\n2. Clears the ADO\n3. Unlocks the mutex\n\nThat should avoid any possibility of overwriting an unsent alert or sending a corrupt alert, allow the caller to handle a failure to send an alert if it wants to, and keep the synchronization mechanism entirely inside PE code.",
      "parentUuid": "aa4371f0_e0ecabe5",
      "revId": "8b57f03aeb47f305c420d78bc22c575c81aa5df9",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
